var e=JSON.parse(`[{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":1,"q":"With reference to ancient Indian history, consider the following statements regarding the rock-cut caves at Barabar and Nagarjuni hills in Bihar:\\n1. They were excavated primarily during the Mauryan period under the patronage of Ashoka and Dasharatha.\\n2. The caves were dedicated exclusively to Buddhist monks of the Theravada sect.\\n3. The interior walls of these caves are characterized by a glass-like mirror polish.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 3 are correct:</b> The Barabar and Nagarjuni caves represent the earliest surviving rock-cut caves in India, dating to the 3rd century BCE under the patronage of Mauryan Emperor Ashoka and his grandson Dasharatha. The interior walls feature the distinctive high-gloss Mauryan mirror polish.</p><p><b>Statement 2 is incorrect:</b> Inscriptions found at the caves indicate they were dedicated primarily to the ascetics of the heterodox Ajivika sect, not Buddhist monks.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":2,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding critical minerals essential for energy transition:\\n1. Cobalt is predominantly extracted as a byproduct of copper and nickel mining.\\n2. The Democratic Republic of Congo accounts for more than half of the world's mined cobalt output.\\n3. Cobalt is non-recyclable once integrated into lithium-ion battery chemistries.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Over 70% of global cobalt is extracted as a byproduct of copper and nickel operations, with the Democratic Republic of Congo producing roughly 70% of the world's primary cobalt supply.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Cobalt is highly recyclable using pyrometallurgical and hydrometallurgical recycling pathways, recovering high-purity battery-grade cobalt.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":3,"q":"Match the following ecological zones with their respective dominant vegetation forms:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Ecological Zone</th><th>Dominant Vegetation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Shola Forests</td><td>Stunted evergreen montane trees interspersed with grasslands</td></tr><tr><td>2. Terai Region</td><td>Dense moist deciduous and sal forests with tall elephant grass</td></tr><tr><td>3. Thorn Forests</td><td>Xerophytic scrub and thorny species like Acacia and Euphorbia</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three pairs are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Shola Forests:</b> High-altitude stunted montane evergreen forests found in the Nilgiris, Anaimalai, and Palani hills, interspersed with rolling grasslands.</li><li><b>Terai Region:</b> Marshy lowlands of the Indo-Gangetic plain dominated by tall grasses and sal timber.</li><li><b>Thorn Forests:</b> Semi-arid zones with rainfall under 70 cm supporting thorny xerophytes.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":4,"q":"With reference to the Open Market Operations (OMOs) conducted by the Reserve Bank of India, consider the following statements:\\n1. OMOs involve the outright purchase and sale of Government Securities (G-Secs) in the open market.\\n2. The purchase of government securities by the central bank injects liquidity into the banking system.\\n3. OMO operations are exclusively executed through the physical auction of Treasury Bills to retail investors.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Open Market Operations (OMOs) involve the buying and selling of government securities to regulate base money supply and interest rates. Buying G-Secs injects liquidity into commercial banks.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> OMOs are conducted electronically primarily through commercial banks and primary dealers on the RBI's E-Kuber platform, covering dated securities as well as short-term debt.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":5,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the distribution of coral reefs in India:\\n1. The Gulf of Mannar and Andaman & Nicobar Islands host diverse fringing and barrier reef ecosystems.\\n2. Lakshadweep is entirely composed of coral atoll formations.\\n3. Coral reefs are completely absent along the mainland western coast of India due to excessive freshwater discharge from all peninsular rivers.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> The Andaman and Nicobar archipelago and Gulf of Mannar possess extensive fringing reefs, while Lakshadweep is a chain of coral atolls built on subterranean volcanic ridges.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Fringing reefs exist along the mainland western coast in localized zones, notably around the Gulf of Kutch and Malvan (Maharashtra).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":6,"q":"With reference to the classical literature of ancient India, which one of the following plays authored by Kalidasa recounts the mythological love story between King Pururavas and the celestial nymph Urvashi?","options":["Malavikagnimitram","Abhijnanashakuntalam","Vikramorvashiyam","Kumarasambhavam"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 3:</b> <i>Vikramorvashiyam</i> is a five-act Sanskrit drama by Kalidasa narrating the Vedic love story of mortal King Pururavas and the Apsara Urvashi.</p><p><i>Malavikagnimitram</i> deals with Agnimitra and Malavika; <i>Abhijnanashakuntalam</i> with Dushyanta and Shakuntala; and <i>Kumarasambhavam</i> is an epic poem on the birth of Kartikeya.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":7,"q":"Consider the following pairs of mountain passes and the regions they connect:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Mountain Pass</th><th>Regions Connected</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Zoji La</td><td>Srinagar and Leh</td></tr><tr><td>2. Nathu La</td><td>Sikkim and Tibet Autonomous Region</td></tr><tr><td>3. Lipulekh</td><td>Uttarakhand and Kailash-Mansarovar route</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three pairs are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Zoji La:</b> Traverses the Great Himalayan Range connecting the Kashmir Valley (Srinagar) to Ladakh (Dras/Leh).</li><li><b>Nathu La:</b> Located in East Sikkim, acting as a historical spur of the Old Silk Route into Tibet.</li><li><b>Lipulekh:</b> Located at the tri-junction of India (Uttarakhand), Nepal, and Tibet, serving as a primary route for Kailash-Mansarovar Yatra.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":8,"q":"With reference to the constitutional developments during British rule in India, the principle of 'Dyarchy' at the provincial level was first formally introduced by which of the following legislative enactments?","options":["Indian Councils Act, 1892","Indian Councils Act, 1909","Government of India Act, 1919","Government of India Act, 1935"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 3:</b> The Government of India Act, 1919 (Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms) introduced Dyarchy in the provinces by dividing provincial subjects into 'Transferred' (administered by Ministers responsible to the legislature) and 'Reserved' (administered by the Governor and his Executive Council without legislative accountability).</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":9,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the ocean currents of the Atlantic Ocean:\\n1. The Gulf Stream is a warm western boundary current flowing northward along the eastern coast of North America.\\n2. The Benguela Current is a cold eastern boundary current flowing equatorward along the southwestern coast of Africa.\\n3. The Canary Current brings warm equatorial water toward the northwestern coast of Europe.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> The Gulf Stream is an intense warm western boundary current of the North Atlantic gyre. The Benguela Current is a cold eastern boundary current associated with coastal upwelling along Namibia and South Africa.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> The Canary Current is a cold current flowing southward along the northwest coast of Africa. Warm waters are transported to northwestern Europe by the North Atlantic Drift.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":10,"q":"Which of the following international environmental agreements explicitly aims to protect human health and the environment from the adverse effects of Persistent Organic Pollutants (POPs)?","options":["Basel Convention","Rotterdam Convention","Stockholm Convention","Minamata Convention"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 3:</b> The <b>Stockholm Convention on Persistent Organic Pollutants</b> (adopted in 2001) is a global treaty aimed at eliminating or restricting the production and use of persistent organic pollutants (such as DDT, PCBs, and dioxins).</p><p>The Basel Convention deals with transboundary movements of hazardous wastes, Rotterdam covers prior informed consent for hazardous chemicals in trade, and Minamata focuses on mercury emissions.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":11,"q":"With reference to the Indus Valley Civilization, consider the following statements regarding urban settlement layouts:\\n1. Lothal featured a rectangular tidal dockyard constructed of burnt bricks connected to the Sabarmati/Bhogavo river basin.\\n2. Dholavira is distinguished by a tripartite division consisting of a Citadel, Middle Town, and Lower Town.\\n3. Kalibangan is noted for the complete absence of any evidence of fire altars or ploughed fields.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Lothal possessed a massive burnt-brick tidal dockyard. Dholavira in the Rann of Kutch exhibits a unique three-tier urban planning structure fortified with stone masonry.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Kalibangan in Rajasthan has yielded distinct evidence of a ploughed agricultural field (showing criss-cross furrows) as well as a series of brick fire altars.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":12,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the geological phenomenon of Paleomagnetism:\\n1. It studies the record of Earth's magnetic field preserved in magnetic minerals through geological time.\\n2. Symmetrical magnetic striping on either side of mid-ocean ridges provided critical empirical evidence for the theory of seafloor spreading.\\n3. Igneous rocks lose their permanent thermoremanent magnetization when heated above their specific Curie temperature.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b></p><ul><li>Paleomagnetism analyzes remanent magnetization in rocks.</li><li>Vine-Matthews-Morley hypothesis verified seafloor spreading by observing symmetrical magnetic reversal stripes parallel to oceanic ridges.</li><li>When heated past the Curie point (e.g., ~580°C for magnetite), ferromagnetic minerals lose their permanent remanent magnetism.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":13,"q":"In the context of modern Indian economic history, the 'Drain of Wealth' theory formulated by Dadabhai Naoroji encompassed which of the following components?\\n1. Home Charges remitted annually to Britain from Indian revenues.\\n2. Interest payments on foreign capital investments in railways and irrigation.\\n3. Guaranteed profits paid to British private railway companies from the Indian treasury.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three components are correct:</b> Dadabhai Naoroji identified the unrequited unilateral transfer of resources from India to Britain as the 'Drain of Wealth'. This included Home Charges (India Office maintenance, pensions, war debts), guaranteed interest yields on British capital deployed in Indian Railways, and private remittances by colonial officers.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":14,"q":"Consider the following drainage patterns and their geomorphic definitions:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Pattern</th><th>Geomorphic Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Dendritic</td><td>Develops on uniform rock resistance and resembles the branching of tree roots</td></tr><tr><td>2. Radial</td><td>Rivers originate from a central elevated peak and flow outward in all directions</td></tr><tr><td>3. Trellis</td><td>Primary tributaries flow parallel to each other with secondary streams joining at right angles</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three pairs are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Dendritic:</b> Forms in areas of homogeneous lithology, resembling tree branches (e.g., Gangetic plain rivers).</li><li><b>Radial:</b> Develops on domes or volcanic cones where drainage radiates outward (e.g., Amarkantak plateau streams).</li><li><b>Trellis:</b> Forms in folded topography of alternating hard and soft rocks with tributaries joining at right angles.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":15,"q":"With reference to the administrative measures during the Sultanate period, the 'Dagh' (branding of horses) and 'Chehra' (descriptive roll of soldiers) systems were introduced by which Sultan to eliminate military fraud?","options":["Qutb-ud-din Aibak","Shamsuddin Iltutmish","Alauddin Khalji","Firuz Shah Tughlaq"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 3:</b> Alauddin Khalji introduced the <i>Dagh</i> (branding of war horses to prevent substitution with inferior breeds) and <i>Huliya/Chehra</i> (systematic physical descriptions of individual soldiers) to enforce military efficiency and prevent ghost-soldier fraud in the standing army.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":16,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the atmospheric phenomenon of Temperature Inversion:\\n1. It is a condition where air temperature increases with increasing altitude in the troposphere.\\n2. A long winter night, clear skies, and calm air facilitate strong nocturnal ground radiation inversion.\\n3. Temperature inversion enhances vertical convective mixing, thereby clearing ground-level air pollution rapidly.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Temperature inversion reverses the normal environmental lapse rate. It occurs efficiently on clear, calm, long winter nights due to rapid radiative cooling of the ground surface.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Temperature inversion creates strong atmospheric stability, suppressing vertical convective currents and trapping pollutants, smog, and particulate matter near the ground.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":17,"q":"With reference to ancient Indian numismatics, which of the following dynasties was the first to issue gold coins bearing the names and portraits of reigning kings?","options":["Mauryan Empire","Indo-Greeks","Satavahana Dynasty","Gupta Empire"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> The <b>Indo-Greeks</b> (2nd century BCE) were the first rulers in the subcontinent to issue coins that can be definitely attributed to specific monarchs, featuring portraits of kings and bilingual/bi-scriptural legends.</p><p>The Kushans later issued large-scale standardized high-purity gold coinage, and the Guptas perfected artistic gold dinars.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":18,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the agro-climatic requirements for Tea cultivation in India:\\n1. It requires well-drained, deep friable loam rich in organic matter and free from waterlogging.\\n2. Tea bushes thrive best in semi-arid conditions with prolonged spells of dry frost.\\n3. High humidity, heavy morning dews, and evenly distributed rainfall throughout the year favor the continuous growth of tender leaves.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 3 are correct:</b> Tea (Camellia sinensis) requires deep, acidic, well-drained loamy soils (often on hill slopes to avoid root stagnation) and thrives in warm, humid climates with 150–250 cm of well-distributed rainfall and frequent mist/dew.</p><p><b>Statement 2 is incorrect:</b> Tea is highly sensitive to stagnant drought and frost; freezing temperatures cause severe leaf blight and retard vegetative flushes.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":19,"q":"With reference to the Socio-Religious Reform Movements in 19th-century India, consider the following statements regarding the 'Young Bengal Movement':\\n1. It was spearheaded by Henry Louis Vivian Derozio at Hindu College, Calcutta.\\n2. The Derozians drew intellectual inspiration from the ideals of the French Revolution and radical British thought.\\n3. The movement mobilized a large peasant base and successfully organized rural tenancy uprisings across Bengal.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Henry Louis Vivian Derozio inspired his students (the Derozians) to question authority, resist orthodox social taboos, and embrace free thought, liberty, and reason inspired by the French Revolution.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> The Young Bengal movement remained largely an urban intellectual phenomenon among college youth and failed to establish sustained links with the agrarian peasantry or masses.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":20,"q":"Match the following Ramsar Wetland sites with their respective States:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Ramsar Site</th><th>State</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Rudrasagar Lake</td><td>Tripura</td></tr><tr><td>2. Sasthamkotta Lake</td><td>Kerala</td></tr><tr><td>3. Bhoj Wetland</td><td>Madhya Pradesh</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three pairs are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Rudrasagar Lake:</b> A lowland wetland located in Sepahijala, Tripura, famous for the Neermahal palace.</li><li><b>Sasthamkotta Lake:</b> The largest freshwater lake in Kerala, located in Kollam district.</li><li><b>Bhoj Wetland:</b> Comprises the Upper and Lower lakes in Bhopal, Madhya Pradesh.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":21,"q":"With reference to the geological features of oceanic relief, consider the following statements regarding Guyots:\\n1. Guyots are isolated volcanic sea-mounts with flat tops submerged beneath the sea surface.\\n2. Their flat summits are formed primarily by wave erosion when the volcanic peak was at or above sea level prior to subsidence.\\n3. Guyots are found exclusively along the active subduction trenches of the Arctic Ocean.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> A guyot (tablemount) is an extinct submarine volcanic mountain with a flat summit created by subaerial wave planation followed by thermal subsidence of the oceanic lithosphere.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Guyots are widely distributed across major ocean basins, with the highest concentration in the Pacific Ocean (e.g., Darwin Guyot guyot chains).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":22,"q":"In the context of ancient Indian philosophical systems, the 'Syadvada' doctrine—asserting that all metaphysical judgments are relative and conditional—is a foundational epistemological pillar of which tradition?","options":["Charvaka","Jainism","Buddhism","Nyaya"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> <i>Syadvada</i> (the doctrine of conditioned predication) is central to <b>Jaina epistemology</b>, derived from the metaphysical theory of <i>Anekantavada</i> (many-sidedness of reality), asserting that reality is complex and can only be stated through seven modal qualifications (Saptabhangi-naya).</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":23,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the Intertropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ):\\n1. It is a broad trough of low pressure situated in equatorial latitudes where the trade winds converge.\\n2. In the Northern Hemisphere summer, the ITCZ shifts northward over the South Asian landmass, facilitating the onset of the Southwest Monsoon.\\n3. The ITCZ remains stationary over the geographic equator throughout the entire year due to constant solar declination.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> The ITCZ is the equatorial thermal trough where northeast and southeast trade winds meet. Its seasonal migration over northern India (Monsoon Trough) in July creates the low-pressure center that draws the moist oceanic Southwest Monsoon winds.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> The ITCZ migrates seasonally north and south following the apparent movement of the thermal equator and zenith sun.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":24,"q":"With reference to the Indian Freedom Struggle, the 'Deshbandhu' title was popularly conferred upon which of the following nationalist leaders?","options":["Chittaranjan Das","Surendranath Banerjee","Bipin Chandra Pal","Jatindra Mohan Sengupta"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 1:</b> <b>Chittaranjan Das</b> was affectionately known as <i>Deshbandhu</i> ('Friend of the Nation'). He was a leading lawyer, nationalist leader, and co-founder of the Congress-Khilafat Swarajya Party in 1923 alongside Motilal Nehru.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":25,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the characteristics of Mangrove ecosystems in India:\\n1. Mangrove plants possess specialized breathing roots called pneumatophores to cope with anaerobic waterlogged soils.\\n2. Vivipary, where seeds germinate while still attached to the parent tree, is a common reproductive adaptation.\\n3. Sundarbans represents the largest contiguous mangrove forest tract in the world.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b></p><ul><li>Mangroves feature specialized negatively geotropic roots (pneumatophores with lenticels) for gas exchange in saline, oxygen-deficient muds.</li><li>Viviparous germination prevents seeds from being swept away or suffocated in anoxic sediments.</li><li>The Sundarbans delta (spanning India and Bangladesh) is the largest single continuous block of tidal halophytic mangrove forest globally.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":26,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the geopolitical and commercial significance of the Strait of Malacca:\\n1. It connects the Andaman Sea (Indian Ocean) and the South China Sea (Pacific Ocean).\\n2. It is bordered by Indonesia, Malaysia, and Singapore.\\n3. It accommodates more than half of the world's annual maritime crude oil shipments.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> The Strait of Malacca is a critical maritime chokepoint connecting the Indian Ocean (Andaman Sea) to the Pacific Ocean (South China Sea), bounded by the littoral states of Indonesia, Malaysia, and Singapore.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> While it is one of the world's busiest chokepoints carrying roughly 25–30% of global maritime crude oil shipments, it does not account for more than half (the Strait of Hormuz is the world's most critical single crude transit passage).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":27,"q":"With reference to the religious developments in ancient India, consider the following statements regarding Mahayana Buddhism:\\n1. Deification of the Buddha and the concept of Bodhisattvas who postpone their own salvation to help others.\\n2. Adoption of Sanskrit as the primary language for canonical texts and philosophical treatises.\\n3. Rejection of image worship and exclusive focus on symbolic stupa veneration.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Mahayana Buddhism elevated Buddha to a divine status, popularized the Bodhisattva ideal, and adopted Sanskrit (as seen in texts by Nagarjuna, Vasubandhu, and Asanga) over Pali.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Mahayana actively pioneered anthropomorphic image worship and idol iconography (as seen in Gandhara and Mathura art), whereas the rejection of human idols and symbolic focus on stupas/footprints characterized early Theravada/Hinayana traditions.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":28,"q":"Match the following major plate tectonic boundaries with their characteristic geological formations:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Plate Boundary Type</th><th>Geological Landform / Feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Oceanic-Oceanic Convergence</td><td>Island Arcs and Deep Ocean Trenches</td></tr><tr><td>2. Continental-Continental Convergence</td><td>Fold Mountains without significant volcanism</td></tr><tr><td>3. Transform Boundary</td><td>San Andreas Fault System</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three pairs are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Oceanic-Oceanic Convergence:</b> Subduction leads to volcanic island arcs and deep trenches (e.g., Mariana Trench, Aleutian Islands).</li><li><b>Continental-Continental Convergence:</b> Squeezes crust into major fold mountains (e.g., Himalayas) with intense crustal thickening but minimal active volcanic extrusion.</li><li><b>Transform Boundary:</b> Plates slide past horizontally without creating or destroying lithosphere (e.g., San Andreas Fault in California).</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":29,"q":"With reference to the fiscal history of British India, the 'Permanent Settlement' of Bengal introduced by Lord Cornwallis in 1793 had which of the following structural provisions?\\n1. Zamindars were recognized as absolute owners of the land subject to regular payment of a fixed revenue.\\n2. The state demand was fixed at 10/11th of the total rental collection, leaving 1/11th for the Zamindar.\\n3. The Sunset Law provided that if revenue was not deposited by sunset on the specified date, the Zamindari rights were auctioned off.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Lord Cornwallis instituted the Permanent Settlement in Bengal, Bihar, and Orissa in 1793. It converted tax-collecting Zamindars into proprietary landholders, fixed the state revenue permanently at 10/11th of rent (Zamindar retaining 1/11th), and enforced the strict 'Sunset Law' of public auction upon default.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":30,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the phenomenon of Ocean Acidification:\\n1. It is primarily driven by the uptake of anthropogenic carbon dioxide $(CO_2)$ from the atmosphere.\\n2. It decreases the concentration of carbonate ions $(CO_3^{2-})$ in seawater, hindering calcification by marine organisms.\\n3. It causes a rise in the pH of the ocean surface waters toward higher alkalinity.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Dissolution of atmospheric $CO_2$ forms carbonic acid, which releases hydrogen ions $(H^+)$ that bind with available carbonate ions $(CO_3^{2-})$. This reduces carbonate saturation, threatening calcifying species like corals, mollusks, and planktonic pteropods.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Higher hydrogen ion concentration leads to a decline (drop) in oceanic pH, making seawater less alkaline (more acidic), not more alkaline.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":31,"q":"With reference to ancient Indian scientific texts, consider the following treatises and their authors:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Treatise</th><th>Author / Scholar</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Sushruta Samhita</td><td>Sushruta (Plastic & Ophthalmic Surgery)</td></tr><tr><td>2. Panchasiddhantika</td><td>Varahamihira (Astronomy & Astrology)</td></tr><tr><td>3. Brahmasphutasiddhanta</td><td>Brahmagupta (Zero & Negative Numbers)</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three pairs are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Sushruta Samhita:</b> Classic surgical compendium detailing rhinoplasty, cataract extraction, and surgical instruments.</li><li><b>Panchasiddhantika:</b> Astronomical treatise by Varahamihira synthesizing five earlier mathematical astronomy models.</li><li><b>Brahmasphutasiddhanta:</b> Mathematical work by Brahmagupta formally defining arithmetic rules for zero, positive, and negative numbers.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":32,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the physical geography of the Peninsular Plateau of India:\\n1. The Western Ghats are higher in elevation and more continuous than the Eastern Ghats.\\n2. The highest peak of Peninsular India, Anamudi, is situated in the Cardamom Hills.\\n3. The rivers flowing through the Deccan trap region have cut deep V-shaped valleys and wide canyons through soft granite strata.","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement 1 is correct:</b> The Western Ghats form an unbroken escarpment with an average elevation of 900–1600 m, whereas the Eastern Ghats are discontinuous and dissected by major east-flowing peninsular rivers.</p><p><b>Statement 2 is incorrect:</b> Anamudi (2,695 m), the highest peak in peninsular India, is located in the Anaimalai Hills of Kerala, not the Cardamom Hills.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> The Deccan Trap region consists of thick, hard, horizontally bedded basaltic lava sheets (step-like trap formations), not soft granite strata.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":33,"q":"With reference to the modern political organizations in pre-Congress India, consider the following bodies:\\n1. Landholders' Society (1838)\\n2. British Indian Association (1851)\\n3. Indian National Association (1876)\\n\\nWhich of the following represents the correct chronological order of their establishment?","options":["1 &rightarrow; 2 &rightarrow; 3","2 &rightarrow; 1 &rightarrow; 3","3 &rightarrow; 1 &rightarrow; 2","1 &rightarrow; 3 &rightarrow; 2"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 1:</b></p><ul><li><b>Landholders' Society (Zamindari Association):</b> Founded in 1838 in Calcutta by Dwarkanath Tagore and Radhakanta Deb.</li><li><b>British Indian Association:</b> Formed in 1851 by merging the Landholders' Society and Bengal British India Society.</li><li><b>Indian National Association:</b> Founded in 1876 by Surendranath Banerjee and Ananda Mohan Bose to represent broad middle-class political demands.</li></ul></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":34,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the planetary wind systems and Pressure Belts:\\n1. The Subtropical High-Pressure belts (Horse Latitudes) are dynamically induced by the sinking of air masses from the upper troposphere.\\n2. In the Southern Hemisphere, the Westerlies are stronger and more persistent than in the Northern Hemisphere due to the vast expanse of open ocean.\\n3. The Doldrums are characterized by intense horizontal wind shear and severe anti-cyclonic subsidence.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Subtropical highs near 30°–35° N/S are dynamic zones where upper Hadley cell convective air descends. In the Southern Hemisphere, lack of land barriers creates exceptionally powerful Westerlies ('Roaring Forties', 'Furious Fifties').</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> The Doldrums (Equatorial Low) are characterized by calm, light variable breezes and strong vertical convection, rather than horizontal wind shear or anti-cyclonic subsidence.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":35,"q":"With reference to the administrative vocabulary of the Mauryan Empire, what was the primary official function of the 'Samaharta'?","options":["Chief Justice of the criminal courts (Kantakashodhana)","Chief Collector-General responsible for state revenue collection and expenditure budgets","Superintendent of state-run iron foundries and mints","Commander-in-Chief of the frontier cavalry forces"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> In Kautilya's <i>Arthashastra</i>, the <b>Samaharta</b> was the Chief Collector-General of Revenue responsible for assessing, collecting, and maintaining accounts of all state receipts across rural, urban, and mineral sectors.</p><p>The <i>Sannidhata</i> was the Chief Treasurer/Storekeeper, and the <i>Pradeshtri</i> oversaw criminal justice (Kantakashodhana).</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":36,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the physical characteristics of Black Soils (Regur) in India:\\n1. They develop extensive deep cracks during dry hot weather, facilitating a process of self-aeration.\\n2. They are rich in lime, iron, magnesia, and alumina, but generally poor in phosphorus, nitrogen, and organic matter.\\n3. They are derived predominantly from the weathering of intrusive granite-gneiss rock formations.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Black soils (vertisols) exhibit high clay content and swell when wet while developing wide, deep cracks when dry (self-ploughing/aeration). They have high mineral nutrients (iron, lime, potash) but are deficient in nitrogen, phosphorus, and organic humus.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Black soils are derived from the weathering of extrusive basaltic lava rocks of the Deccan Traps, not granite-gneiss basement rocks (which yield Red and Yellow soils).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":37,"q":"With reference to the trade policies of the Vijayanagar Empire, consider the following statements regarding the import of war horses:\\n1. Vijayanagar rulers maintained a royal state monopoly on the purchase of high-grade Arabian and Persian horses.\\n2. Krishnadevaraya established close commercial and diplomatic ties with the Portuguese to secure exclusive control over the horse trade arriving at western ports.\\n3. The Vijayanagar cavalry relied exclusively on locally bred Deccan ponies due to a complete embargo on imported horses from the Persian Gulf.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> High-quality Arabian and Persian horses were vital for military superiority over the Deccan Sultanates. Krishnadevaraya maintained cordial relations with Portuguese governor Afonso de Albuquerque to secure exclusive rights over horse imports through Goa and Bhatkal.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Local Indian climates were poorly suited for breeding war horses, making continuous foreign imports from Arabia and Persia indispensable to the Vijayanagar state.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":38,"q":"Match the following Indian river basins with their characteristic waterfalls:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Waterfall</th><th>River System</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Jog Falls (Gerosoppa)</td><td>Sharavathi River</td></tr><tr><td>2. Dhuandhar Falls</td><td>Narmada River</td></tr><tr><td>3. Hundru Falls</td><td>Subarnarekha River</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three pairs are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Jog Falls:</b> Located on the Sharavathi River in Shivamogga district, Karnataka (one of India's highest plunge falls).</li><li><b>Dhuandhar Falls:</b> Formed by the Narmada River carving through marble rocks in Bhedaghat, Jabalpur (Madhya Pradesh).</li><li><b>Hundru Falls:</b> Formed on the Subarnarekha River on the Ranchi Plateau in Jharkhand.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":39,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the Indian National Army (INA) and its trials in 1945–46:\\n1. The historic joint public trial of Prem Sahgal, Gurbaksh Singh Dhillon, and Shah Nawaz Khan was held at the Red Fort, Delhi.\\n2. The Congress constituted an INA Defence Committee comprising prominent lawyers including Bhulabhai Desai, Tej Bahadur Sapru, and Jawaharlal Nehru.\\n3. The Muslim League and the Communist Party of India completely boycotted the nationwide campaign for the release of INA prisoners.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> The first Red Fort trial involved three INA officers representing Hindu, Muslim, and Sikh communities. The legal defense team was headed by Bhulabhai Desai, supported by Tej Bahadur Sapru, K.N. Katju, and Jawaharlal Nehru.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> The INA defense movement received unprecedented cross-party support; the Muslim League, CPI, Akalis, and Justice Party all joined public relief and legal agitation efforts.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":40,"q":"With reference to the physical mechanisms governing the Indian Summer Monsoon, the 'Somali Jet' (Findlater Jet) is best described as which of the following?","options":["A high-altitude upper tropospheric easterly jet stream flowing south of the Tibetan Plateau.","A low-level cross-equatorial oceanic jet stream that accelerates southwest monsoonal flow across the Arabian Sea toward the Indian peninsula.","A polar westerly jet stream causing winter depressions over the northwest plains.","A localized katabatic wind descending from the East African rift escarpments during spring."],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> The <b>Somali Jet</b> (Findlater Jet) is a low-level cross-equatorial wind surge that flows off the coast of East Africa (Somalia) and turns eastward over the Arabian Sea, functioning as a primary low-level moisture conduit that strengthens the Indian summer monsoon.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":41,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'Lothal' archaeological site of the Indus Valley Civilization:\\n1. It is situated along the Bhogavo river, a tributary of the Sabarmati in Gujarat.\\n2. Excavations have revealed a double burial where a male and female were interred together.\\n3. Persian Gulf seal finds at the site provide archaeological evidence of maritime trade with contemporary Mesopotamia.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Lothal is located on the Bhogavo river in Gujarat. Excavations by S.R. Rao revealed an advanced tidal dockyard, bead factories, joint male-female double burials, and circular Persian Gulf button seals proving direct overseas trade linkages.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":42,"q":"With reference to the modern agrarian movements in India, the 'Bardoli Satyagraha' of 1928 was launched primarily to protest against which of the following?","options":["The arbitrary 22% enhancement of land revenue rates by the Bombay Provincial Government.","The forced cultivation of indigo under the Tinkathia system.","The imposition of forest grazing taxes on tribal communities in the Dangs.","The eviction of sharecroppers by private European sugar mills."],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 1:</b> The <b>Bardoli Satyagraha (1928)</b> in Surat district, Gujarat, was led by Vallabhbhai Patel (where he earned the title 'Sardar') against the unjust 22% hike in land revenue assessments imposed following a flawed land revenue reassessment.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":43,"q":"Consider the following pairs of mountain peaks and their respective ranges in the Himalayas:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Mountain Peak</th><th>Himalayan Range / Section</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Nanda Devi</td><td>Garhwal Himalayas (Uttarakhand)</td></tr><tr><td>2. Kanchenjunga</td><td>Sikkim Himalayas</td></tr><tr><td>3. Kamet</td><td>Zaskar Range</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three pairs are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Nanda Devi (7,816 m):</b> Second highest peak in India, located entirely in the Garhwal Himalayas of Uttarakhand.</li><li><b>Kanchenjunga (8,586 m):</b> Located on the border between Sikkim and Nepal.</li><li><b>Kamet (7,756 m):</b> Second highest peak in the Garhwal/Zaskar mountain belt in Chamoli, Uttarakhand.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":44,"q":"With reference to the ancient administration of the Gupta Empire, the term 'Agrahara' referred to which of the following categories of land grants?","options":["Crown lands reserved exclusively for maintenance of the royal elephant stables.","Tax-free perpetual land grants bestowed upon Brahmins and religious institutions.","Waste lands leased temporarily to foreign corporate guilds for timber extraction.","Frontier military cantonment lands assigned to garrison commanders."],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> In the Gupta and post-Gupta periods, <b>Agrahara</b> (or <i>Brahmadeya</i>) was a tax-exempt, perpetual land endowment made to Brahmins, temples, or monasteries, transferring fiscal collection rights and administrative immunities to the donees.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":45,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the geological formation and spatial distribution of Bauxite in India:\\n1. Bauxite is formed by the intense tertiary tropical weathering and leaching of aluminous silicate rocks.\\n2. Odisha is the leading bauxite-producing state, accounting for more than half of India's total output.\\n3. The Panchpatmali deposits in Koraput district represent one of the largest bauxite reserves in India.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Bauxite deposits are residual weathering products of rocks rich in aluminum silicates under tropical conditions with alternating wet and dry seasons (lateritization). Odisha holds over 50% of national reserves, with Panchpatmali in Koraput being the primary supply deposit for NALCO's operations.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":46,"q":"With reference to the revolutionary nationalist movement in India, who among the following established the 'Abhinav Bharat Society' in 1904 as a secret revolutionary organization?","options":["Vinayak Damodar Savarkar","Barindra Kumar Ghosh","Rash Behari Bose","Sachindra Nath Sanyal"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 1:</b> <b>Vinayak Damodar Savarkar</b> along with his brother Ganesh Savarkar founded the <i>Mitra Mela</i> in Nashik in 1899, which was formally reorganized as the underground revolutionary society <b>Abhinav Bharat</b> in 1904.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":47,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the characteristics of Tropical Cyclones in the North Indian Ocean (Bay of Bengal and Arabian Sea):\\n1. Sea surface temperatures exceeding $27^\\\\circ\\\\text{C}$ and significant Coriolis force are essential prerequisites for their cyclogenesis.\\n2. Tropical cyclones exhibit a calm, descending-air central zone called the 'Eye', bounded by the eyewall with the highest sustained wind velocities.\\n3. Cyclones originating in the Bay of Bengal are steered toward the Indian coast primarily by low-level westerly monsoon jets.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Warm oceanic waters $(\\\\ge 27^\\\\circ\\\\text{C})$, high humidity, low vertical wind shear, and sufficient Coriolis force (away from 0°–5° latitude) sustain tropical cyclogenesis. The Eye is characterized by subsidence and clear skies, while the eyewall contains the most violent convection.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Tropical cyclones are steered by upper-tropospheric easterly steering winds and subtropical anticyclonic ridges, not low-level westerly monsoon jets.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":48,"q":"With reference to the Bhakti literature of medieval South India, the sacred devotional anthology titled 'Divya Prabandham' compiles the devotional hymns composed by which of the following saint groups?","options":["Nayanars (Saivite Saints)","Alvars (Vaishnavite Saints)","Virasaivas (Lingayats)","Siddhas (Tamil Yogis)"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> The <b>Nalayira Divya Prabandham</b> is a canonical collection of 4,000 Tamil verses composed by the 12 <b>Alvars</b> (Vaishnavite mystic saints, including Nammalvar and Andal), compiled in the 10th century by Nathamuni.</p><p>The canonical collection of the Saivite Nayanars is the <i>Tirumurai</i> (which includes the <i>Tevaram</i>).</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":49,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the physical geography of coral atolls:\\n1. An atoll is a ring-shaped coral reef island that encircles a central body of water called a lagoon.\\n2. According to Charles Darwin's subsidence theory, atolls evolve sequentially from fringing reefs to barrier reefs and finally into atolls as the underlying volcanic island subsides.\\n3. The Maldives, Lakshadweep, and Chagos archipelagos are built along the submerged Chagos-Laccadive submarine ridge.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Atolls consist of ring-like biogenic carbonate reefs surrounding a lagoon. Charles Darwin's subsidence theory explains their evolution from fringing reefs around subsiding oceanic volcanic cones. Lakshadweep, Maldives, and Chagos are atoll chains atop the Chagos-Laccadive volcanic plateau.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":50,"q":"With reference to the administrative reforms during British rule, the 'Lee Commission' of 1923–24 was appointed to make recommendations regarding which of the following areas?","options":["Financial decentralization and provincial taxation models","Structure, recruitment, and conditions of service of the Superior Civil Services in India","Reorganization of Indian railway management under state ownership","Establishment of uniform criminal legal codes across princely states"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> The <b>Royal Commission on the Superior Civil Services in India (Lee Commission, 1923–24)</b> recommended that the Indian Civil Service (ICS), Indian Police Service (IPS), and Indian Forest Service (IFS) be retained as All-India Services with a 50:50 ratio between Europeans and Indians within 15 years, and urged the immediate establishment of the Public Service Commission (which was set up in 1926).</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":51,"q":"Consider the following types of vehicles:\\nI. Full battery electric vehicles\\nII. Hydrogen fuel cell vehicles\\nIII. Fuel cell-electric hybrid vehicles\\n\\nHow many of the above are considered as alternative powertrain vehicles?","options":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three are alternative powertrains:</b> Alternative powertrains include advanced propulsion technologies that do not rely exclusively on internal combustion engines.</p><p>This incorporates battery electric vehicles (BEVs), fuel cell electric vehicles (FCEVs), and plug-in hybrid electric configurations.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":52,"q":"With reference to Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), consider the following statements:\\nI. All types of UAVs can do vertical landing.\\nII. All types of UAVs can do automated hovering.\\nIII. All types of UAVs can use battery only as a source of power supply.\\n\\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","options":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All statements are incorrect:</b></p><ul><li>Fixed-wing UAVs require runways/launchers and cannot perform vertical landings or automated hovering, unlike rotary-wing drones.</li><li>UAVs utilize diverse power sources including internal combustion engines, hydrogen fuel cells, and hybrid setups, not just batteries.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":53,"q":"In the context of electric vehicle batteries, consider the following elements:\\n1. Cobalt\\n2. Graphite\\n3. Lithium\\n4. Nickel\\n\\nHow many of the above usually make up battery cathodes?","options":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All the four"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Three elements (Lithium, Nickel, Cobalt) make up typical cathodes:</b> Modern EV chemistries (like NMC and NCA) utilize Lithium, Nickel, and Cobalt in their cathode structures.</p><p>Graphite is predominantly employed as the anode material.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":54,"q":"Consider the following:\\n1. Cigarette butts\\n2. Eyeglass lenses\\n3. Car tyres\\n\\nHow many of them contain plastic?","options":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three contain plastic polymers:</b> Cigarette filters contain cellulose acetate (a thermoplastic). Most modern eyeglass lenses are made from optical plastics (polycarbonate/CR-39). Car tyres incorporate synthetic plastic polymers and are major sources of environmental microplastics.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":55,"q":"Coal gasification technology can be used in the production of how many of the following?\\nI. Ethanol\\nII. Nitroglycerine\\nIII. Urea","options":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Two products (Ethanol and Urea) are produced via syngas:</b> Syngas $(CO + H_2)$ from coal gasification is catalytic synthesized into methanol/ethanol and converted into ammonia to manufacture urea fertilizer.</p><p>Nitroglycerine is synthesized via nitration of glycerol, not direct coal gasification.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":56,"q":"What is the common characteristic of the chemical substances generally known as CL-20, HMX and LLM-105, which are sometimes talked about in media?","options":["These are alternatives to hydrofluorocarbon refrigerants","These are explosives in military weapons","These are high-energy fuels for cruise missiles","These are fuels for rocket propulsion"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> CL-20, HMX (Octogen), and LLM-105 are high-density energetic chemical explosive formulations developed for advanced conventional warheads and military demolitions.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":57,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nI. It is expected that Majorana 1 chip will enable quantum computing.\\nII. Majorana 1 chip has been introduced by Amazon Web Services (AWS).\\nIII. Deep learning is a subset of machine learning.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements I and III are correct:</b> Majorana 1 is an advanced topological qubit quantum computing chip architecture. Deep learning is a specialized subset of machine learning using multi-layer neural networks.</p><p><b>Statement II is incorrect:</b> The Majorana 1 chip was developed and introduced by Microsoft, not AWS.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":58,"q":"With reference to monoclonal antibodies, often mentioned in news, consider the following statements:\\nI. They are man-made proteins.\\nII. They stimulate immunological function due to their ability to bind to specific antigens.\\nIII. They are used in treating viral infections like that of Nipah virus.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Monoclonal antibodies (mAbs) are lab-engineered cloning proteins designed to bind target antigens. Monoclonal therapies like m102.4 are deployed experimentally against lethal viral pathogens like Nipah and Hendra.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":59,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nI. No virus can survive in ocean water.\\nII. No virus can infect bacteria.\\nIII. No virus can change the cellular transcriptional activity in host cells.\\n\\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","options":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All statements are incorrect:</b></p><ul><li>Viruses are the most abundant biological entities in marine and oceanic ecosystems.</li><li>Bacteriophages are viruses that specifically infect and replicate within bacteria.</li><li>Viruses hijack and alter host cellular transcription machinery to synthesize viral genomes and proteins.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":60,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nStatement I: Activated carbon is a good and an attractive tool to remove pollutants from effluent streams and to remediate contaminants from various industries.\\nStatement II: Activated carbon exhibits a large surface area and a strong potential for adsorbing heavy metals.\\nStatement III: Activated carbon can be easily synthesized from environmental wastes with high carbon content.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","options":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and Statement III is correct and that explain statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both statements explain Statement I:</b> Statement II explains performance efficiency (porous high surface-area adsorption of toxins/heavy metals), while Statement III explains economic attractiveness (synthesis from agricultural/biomass waste).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":61,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nStatement I: Studies indicate that carbon dioxide emissions from cement industry account for more than 5% of global carbon emissions.\\nStatement II: Silica-bearing clay is mixed with limestone while manufacturing cement.\\nStatement III: Limestone is converted into lime during clinker production for cement manufacturing.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","options":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement III explains the massive $CO_2$ footprint:</b> Chemical calcination of limestone $(CaCO_3 \\rightarrow CaO + CO_2)$ during clinker processing accounts for over 50% of the cement sector's direct emissions, making Statement III the primary explanation. Statement II is factually correct as a raw material mixing step but does not explain the high emissions profile.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":62,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nStatement I: At the 28th United Nations Climate Change Conference (COP28), India refrained from signing the 'Declaration on Climate and Health'.\\nStatement II: The COP28 Declaration on Climate and Health is a binding declaration; and if signed, it becomes mandatory to decarbonize the health sector.\\nStatement III: If India's health sector is decarbonized, the resilience of its health-care system may be compromised.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","options":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement II is incorrect and Statement III explains Statement I:</b> The declaration is a voluntary non-binding pledge, making Statement II false. India refrained due to practical concerns that premature decarbonization mandates on healthcare cooling and infrastructure could compromise healthcare access and resilience in developing regions.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":63,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nStatement I: Scientific studies suggest that a shift is taking place in the Earth's rotation and axis.\\nStatement II: Solar flares and associated coronal mass ejections bombarded the Earth's outermost atmosphere with tremendous amount of energy.\\nStatement III: As the Earth's polar ice melts, the water tends to move towards the equator.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","options":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both II and III are correct, but only III explains the rotational shift:</b> Melting glaciers redistribute mass from high latitudes toward equatorial oceans, altering Earth's moment of inertia and true polar wander. Solar flares impact space weather and geomagnetism but do not alter Earth's planetary spin axis.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":64,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nStatement I: Article 6 of the Paris Agreement on climate change is frequently discussed in global discussions on sustainable development and climate change.\\nStatement II: Article 6 of the Paris Agreement on climate change sets out the principles of carbon markets.\\nStatement III: Article 6 of the Paris Agreement on climate change intends to promote inter-country non-market strategies to reach their climate targets.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","options":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both statements explain Statement I:</b> Article 6 governs bilateral carbon trading (Article 6.2), centralized crediting mechanisms (Article 6.4), and non-market collaborative approaches (Article 6.8), making both statements foundational explanations.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":65,"q":"Which one of the following launched the 'Nature Solutions Finance Hub for Asia and the Pacific'?","options":["The Asian Development Bank (ADB)","The Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB)","The New Development Bank (NDB)","The International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD)"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 1:</b> The <b>Asian Development Bank (ADB)</b> launched the Nature Solutions Finance Hub at COP28 to mobilize capital and scale up investments in nature-based climate adaptation solutions across the Asia-Pacific region.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":66,"q":"With reference to 'Direct Air Capture' (DAC), which of the following statements is/are correct?\\nI. It can be used as a way of carbon sequestration.\\nII. It can be a valuable approach for plastic production and in food processing.\\nIII. In aviation, it can be a source of carbon for combining with hydrogen to create synthetic low-carbon fuel.","options":["I and II only","III only","I, II and III","None of the above statements is correct"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Direct Air Capture extracts $CO_2$ directly from ambient air. Captured carbon can be sequestered underground, utilized as industrial feedstocks in chemicals/polymers/food carbonation, or synthesized with clean hydrogen into Sustainable Aviation Fuels (SAF).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":67,"q":"Regarding Peacock tarantula (Gooty tarantula), consider the following statements:\\nI. It is an omnivorous crustacean.\\nII. Its natural habitat in India is only limited to some forest areas.\\nIII. In its natural habitat, it is an arboreal species.","options":["I only","I and III","II only","II and III"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements II and III are correct:</b> The Peacock tarantula <i>(Poecilotheria metallica)</i> is critically endangered, endemic to a localized deciduous forest tract in Andhra Pradesh (Eastern Ghats), and lives predominantly in tree hollows (arboreal).</p><p><b>Statement I is incorrect:</b> It is a predatory carnivorous arachnid (spider), not a crustacean.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":68,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nI. Carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions in India are less than 0.5 t CO2/ capita.\\nII. In terms of CO2 emissions from fuel combustion, India ranks second in the Asia-Pacific region.\\nIII. Electricity and heat producers are the largest sources of CO2 emissions in India.","options":["I and III only","II only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements II and III are correct:</b> India is the second-largest emitter in the Asia-Pacific (behind China), and the thermal power/electricity generation sector accounts for more than half of domestic emissions.</p><p><b>Statement I is incorrect:</b> India's per capita carbon emissions stand around ~1.9 to 2.0 tonnes $CO_2$, significantly higher than 0.5 tonnes.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":69,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\\nI. Cassava : Woody shrub\\nII. Ginger : Herb with pseudostem\\nIII. Malabar spinach : Herbaceous climber\\nIV. Mint : Annual shrub\\nV. Papaya : Woody shrub\\n\\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","options":["Only two","Only three","Only four","All the five"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Three pairs (I, II, and III) are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li>Cassava is a perennial woody shrub.</li><li>Ginger is an herbaceous perennial featuring pseudostems formed by leaf sheaths.</li><li>Malabar spinach (Basella alba) is a succulent herbaceous climbing vine.</li><li>Mint is an aromatic perennial herb (not a shrub), and papaya is a large soft-wooded/herbaceous arborescent plant.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":70,"q":"With reference to the planet Earth, consider the following statements:\\nI. Rain forests produce more oxygen than that produced by oceans.\\nII. Marine phytoplankton and photosynthetic bacteria produce about 50% of the world's oxygen.\\nIII. Well-oxygenated surface water contains several folds higher oxygen than that in atmospheric air.","options":["I and II","II only","I and III","None of the above statements is correct"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement II is correct:</b> Marine phytoplankton and cyanobacteria generate approximately 50% or more of global atmospheric oxygen via photosynthesis.</p><p><b>Statements I and III are incorrect:</b> Oceans produce more total net oxygen than tropical rainforests. Dissolved oxygen concentration in water (approx. 8–10 mg/L) is vastly lower than oxygen availability in ambient atmospheric air (~210,000 mg/L).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":71,"q":"Consider the following statements about Raja Ram Mohan Roy:\\nI. He possessed great love and respect for the traditional philosophical systems of the East.\\nII. He desired his countrymen to accept the rational and scientific approach and the principle of human dignity and social equality of all men and women.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both statements are correct:</b> Raja Ram Mohan Roy deeply studied and respected the monotheistic philosophical core of the Upanishads while vigorously championing modern scientific rationalism, human dignity, and women's rights.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":72,"q":"Consider the following subjects with regard to Non-Cooperation Programme:\\nI. Boycott of law-courts and foreign cloth\\nII. Observance of strict non-violence\\nIII. Retention of titles and honours without using them in public\\nIV. Establishment of Panchayats for settling disputes\\n\\nHow many of the above were parts of the Non-Cooperation Programme?","options":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All the four"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Three items (I, II, and IV) were core elements:</b> The programme mandated boycotting British courts/goods, maintaining strict non-violence, and establishing indigenous arbitration panchayats.</p><p>Item III is incorrect because it mandated the total surrender/renunciation of titles and honorary posts, not their silent retention.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":73,"q":"The irrigation device called 'Araghatta' was","options":["A water bag made of leather pulled over a pulley","A large wheel with earthen pots tied to the outer ends of its spokes","A larger earthen pot driven by bullocks","A large water bucket pulled up by rope directly by hand"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> An <b>Araghatta</b> (Persian wheel precursor) was an ancient mechanical water-lifting wheel fitted with spoke-mounted earthen pots (ghatiyantra) to irrigate fields from wells.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":74,"q":"Who among the following rulers in ancient India had assumed the titles 'Mattavilasa', 'Vichitrachitta' and 'Gunabhara'?","options":["Mahendravarman I","Simhavishnu","Narasimhavarman I","Simhavarman"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 1:</b> The Pallava monarch <b>Mahendravarman I</b> (590–630 CE) assumed the titles <i>Mattavilasa</i> (author of the farce Mattavilasa Prahasana), <i>Vichitrachitta</i> (curious-minded innovator of rock temples), and <i>Gunabhara</i>.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":75,"q":"Fa-hien (Faxian), the Chinese pilgrim, travelled to India during the reign of","options":["Samudragupta","Chandragupta II","Kumaragupta I","Skandagupta"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> Fa-hien visited India during the golden age reign of <b>Chandragupta II Vikramaditya</b> (c. 399–412 CE) to collect sacred Buddhist Vinaya manuscripts.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":76,"q":"Who among the following led a successful military campaign against the kingdom of Srivijaya, the powerful maritime State, which ruled the Malay Peninsula, Sumatra, Java and the neighbouring islands?","options":["Amoghavarsha (Rashtrakuta)","Prataparudra (Kakatiya)","Rajendra I (Chola)","Vishnuvardhana (Hoysala)"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 3:</b> Rajendra Chola I led a successful and historic naval expedition against the maritime Srivijaya Empire in 1025 CE, securing commercial sea lanes to China.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":77,"q":"With reference to ancient India (600-322 BC), consider the following pairs:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Mahajanapada</th><th>Associated River</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Asmaka</td><td>Godavari</td></tr><tr><td>2. Kamboja</td><td>Vipas</td></tr><tr><td>3. Avanti</td><td>Mahanadi</td></tr><tr><td>4. Kosala</td><td>Sarayu</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nHow many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All the four"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Only two pairs are correctly matched:</b> Asmaka was situated along the Godavari, and Kosala was associated with the Sarayu river.</p><p>Kamboja was located in the northwest (Hindukush/Pamirs), while Vipas (Beas) is in Punjab. Avanti was centered around the Chambal/Betwa river basins in Malwa, not the Mahanadi.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":78,"q":"The first Gandharva Mahavidyalaya, a music training school, was set up in 1901 by Vishnu Digambar Paluskar in","options":["Delhi","Gwalior","Ujjain","Lahore"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 4:</b> The first Gandharva Mahavidyalaya was established by Pandit Vishnu Digambar Paluskar on 5 May 1901 in Lahore to democratize Hindustani classical music education.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":79,"q":"Ashokan inscriptions suggest that the 'Pradeshika', 'Rajuka' and 'Yukta' were important officers at the","options":["village-level administration","district-level administration","provincial administration","level of the central administration"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> Major Rock Edict III mentions Pradeshikas (overall district administrators), Rajukas (revenue and judicial officers), and Yuktas (secretarial and accounting staff) as district-level officers who undertook tours every five years.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":80,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of the Non-Cooperation Movement:\\nI. The Congress declared the attainment of 'Swaraj' by all legitimate and peaceful means to be its objective.\\nII. It was to be implemented in stages with civil disobedience and non-payment of taxes for the next stage only if 'Swaraj' did not come within a year and the Government resorted to repression.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both statements are correct:</b> At the Nagpur session (1920), the Congress redefined its creed to the attainment of Swaraj by peaceful and legitimate means. Gandhiji planned for the movement to progress in stages, reserving full civil disobedience and tax refusal for subsequent phases if repression continued.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":81,"q":"With reference to investments, consider the following:\\nI. Bonds\\nII. Hedge Funds\\nIII. Stocks\\nIV. Venture Capital\\n\\nHow many of the above are treated as Alternative Investment Funds?","options":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All the four"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Only two (Hedge Funds and Venture Capital) are classified as AIFs:</b> Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs) are pooled investment vehicles distinct from traditional investment assets like publicly traded stocks and standard bonds.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":82,"q":"Which of the following are the sources of income for the Reserve Bank of India?\\nI. Buying and selling Government bonds\\nII. Buying and selling foreign currency\\nIII. Pension fund management\\nIV. Lending to private companies\\nV. Printing and distributing currency notes","options":["I and II only","II, III and IV","I, III, IV and V","I, II and V"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements I and II are the primary income operations:</b> RBI generates income through open market trading/coupon interest on government securities, and foreign exchange market transactions.</p><p>RBI does not manage private pension funds, lend directly to corporate commercial firms, or record pure note distribution as direct commercial income.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":83,"q":"With reference to the Government of India, consider the following information:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Agency / Directorate</th><th>Core Mandate</th><th>Ministry</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Directorate of Enforcement</td><td>Enforcement of Fugitive Economic Offenders Act</td><td>Ministry of Home Affairs</td></tr><tr><td>2. Directorate of Revenue Intelligence</td><td>Enforces Customs Act, 1962</td><td>Ministry of Finance</td></tr><tr><td>3. Directorate General of Systems and Data Management</td><td>Big data analytics for tax administration</td><td>Ministry of Finance</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nIn how many of the above rows is the information correctly matched?","options":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Only two rows are correctly matched:</b> The Directorate of Enforcement (ED) functions under the Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance (not Home Affairs). DRI and DG Systems & Data Management are both correctly placed under the Ministry of Finance.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":84,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nI. The Reserve Bank of India mandates all the listed companies in India to submit a Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report (BRSR).\\nII. In India, a company submitting a BRSR makes disclosures in the report that are largely non-financial in nature.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement II is correct:</b> BRSR reporting involves non-financial environmental, social, and governance (ESG) disclosures.</p><p><b>Statement I is incorrect:</b> The mandate is issued by SEBI (Securities and Exchange Board of India) for the top 1,000 listed entities by market capitalization, not the RBI.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":85,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nStatement I: In India, income from allied agricultural activities like poultry farming and wool rearing in rural areas is exempted from any tax.\\nStatement II: In India, rural agricultural land is not considered a capital asset under the provisions of the Income-tax Act, 1961.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I","Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct","Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement I is not correct:</b> Income from allied activities such as poultry farming, dairy, and livestock rearing is treated as commercial business income and is not fully tax-exempt like primary cultivation.</p><p><b>Statement II is correct:</b> Under Section 2(14) of the Income-tax Act, rural agricultural land is excluded from the definition of a capital asset.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":86,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nI. India has joined the Minerals Security Partnership as a member.\\nII. India is a resource-rich country in all the 30 critical minerals that it has identified.\\nIII. The Parliament in 2023 has amended the Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act, 1957 empowering the Central Government to exclusively auction mining lease and composite license for certain critical minerals.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements I and III are correct:</b> India joined the US-led Minerals Security Partnership (MSP) in 2023. The MMDR Amendment Act, 2023 empowered the Central Government to auction composite licenses for critical and strategic minerals.</p><p><b>Statement II is incorrect:</b> India is heavily import-dependent on several key critical minerals like lithium, cobalt, and nickel.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":87,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nStatement I: As regards returns from an investment in a company, generally, bondholders are considered to be relatively at lower risk than stockholders.\\nStatement II: Bondholders are lenders to a company whereas stockholders are its owners.\\nStatement III: For repayment purpose, bondholders are prioritized over stockholders by a company.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","options":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement I explains Statement II","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both statements explain Statement I:</b> Bondholders hold debt claims as creditors (Statement II) and enjoy senior priority in claims on assets and cash flows during liquidation over equity shareholders (Statement III), making their investments lower risk.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":88,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nI. India accounts for a very large portion of all equity option contracts traded globally thus exhibiting a great boom.\\nII. India's stock market has grown rapidly in the recent past even overtaking Hong Kong's at some point of time.\\nIII. There is no regulatory body either to warn the small investors about the risks of options trading or to act on unregistered financial advisors in this regard.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements I and II are correct:</b> Indian exchanges (NSE) dominate global volumes in equity index options. India's market capitalization surpassed Hong Kong's in early 2024 to become the fourth-largest equity market globally.</p><p><b>Statement III is incorrect:</b> SEBI actively regulates derivatives, publishes risk disclosure metrics, and penalizes unregistered financial advisors.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":89,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nStatement I: Circular economy reduces the emissions of greenhouse gases.\\nStatement II: Circular economy reduces the use of raw materials as inputs.\\nStatement III: Circular economy reduces wastage in the production process.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","options":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both statements explain Statement I:</b> Minimizing virgin material extraction (Statement II) and closing resource loops through recycling/waste prevention (Statement III) directly reduce energy consumption and embedded emissions.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":90,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nI. Capital receipts create a liability or cause a reduction in the assets of the Government.\\nII. Borrowings and disinvestment are capital receipts.\\nIII. Interest received on loans creates a liability of the Government.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements I and II are correct:</b> Capital receipts either incur debt liabilities (market borrowings) or liquidate assets (disinvestment proceeds).</p><p><b>Statement III is incorrect:</b> Interest received on loans given by the government is classified as a non-tax revenue receipt, not a liability.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":91,"q":"Consider the following countries:\\nI. Austria\\nII. Bulgaria\\nIII. Croatia\\nIV. Serbia\\nV. Sweden\\nVI. North Macedonia\\n\\nHow many of the above are members of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization?","options":["Only three","Only four","Only five","All the six"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Only four are NATO members:</b> Bulgaria, Croatia, North Macedonia, and Sweden are member states. Austria and Serbia maintain formal military neutrality.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":92,"q":"Consider the following countries:\\nI. Bolivia\\nII. Brazil\\nIII. Colombia\\nIV. Ecuador\\nV. Paraguay\\nVI. Venezuela\\n\\nAndes mountains pass through how many of the above countries?","options":["Only two","Only three","Only four","Only five"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Only four countries:</b> The Andes mountain chain traverses seven South American nations: Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Bolivia, Chile, and Argentina. From the given list, it passes through Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador, and Venezuela (not Brazil or Paraguay).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":93,"q":"Consider the following water bodies:\\nI. Lake Tanganyika\\nII. Lake Tonle Sap\\nIII. Patos Lagoon\\n\\nThrough how many of them does the equator pass?","options":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>The equator passes through none of them:</b> Lake Tanganyika lies entirely south of the equator in East Africa; Lake Tonle Sap is located north of the equator in Cambodia; and Lagoa dos Patos is in southern Brazil.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":94,"q":"Consider the following statements about turmeric during the year 2022-23:\\nI. India is the largest producer and exporter of turmeric in the world.\\nII. More than 30 varieties of turmeric are grown in India.\\nIII. Maharashtra, Telangana, Karnataka and Tamil Nadu are major turmeric producing States in India.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> India leads global production and export of turmeric, cultivates over 30 recognized agro-varieties, and concentrates production in Maharashtra, Telangana, Karnataka, and Tamil Nadu.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":95,"q":"Which of the following are the evidences of the phenomenon of continental drift?\\nI. The belt of ancient rocks from Brazil coast matches with those from Western Africa.\\nII. The gold deposits of Ghana are derived from the Brazil plateau when the two continents lay side by side.\\nIII. The Gondwana system of sediments from India is known to have its counterparts in six different landmasses of the Southern Hemisphere.\\n\\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","options":["I and III only","I and II only","I, II and III","II and III only"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements provide empirical evidence:</b> Alfred Wegener cited matching orogenic belts (Brazil and West Africa), rich placer gold deposits in Ghana originating from Brazilian source veins, and identical glacial tillite sequences in Gondwana strata across South America, Africa, Madagascar, India, Australia, and Antarctica.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":96,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nStatement I: The amount of dust particles in the atmosphere is more in subtropical and temperate areas than in equatorial and polar regions.\\nStatement II: Subtropical and temperate areas have less dry winds.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I","Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct","Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement I is correct:</b> Dust particle concentrations are highest in subtropical and temperate desert regions due to strong dry winds and low precipitation wash-out.</p><p><b>Statement II is not correct:</b> Subtropical desert and temperate continental belts experience higher occurrences of dry winds, not fewer.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":97,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nStatement I: In January, in the Northern Hemisphere, the isotherms bend equatorward while crossing the landmasses, and poleward while crossing the oceans.\\nStatement II: In January, the air over the oceans is warmer than that over the landmasses in the Northern Hemisphere.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I","Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct","Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both statements are correct and Statement II explains Statement I:</b> Due to the differential heat capacity of land and water, oceans retain heat in winter, making the overlying air warmer than cold continental landmasses, causing isotherms to bend poleward over oceans and equatorward over land.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":98,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nStatement I: In the context of effect of water on rocks, chalk is known as a very permeable rock whereas clay is known as quite an impermeable or least permeable rock.\\nStatement II: Chalk is porous and hence can absorb water.\\nStatement III: Clay is not at all porous.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","options":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Only Statement II explains Statement I:</b> Chalk has high porosity and interconnected fissures making it permeable. Clay has high microscopic porosity but extremely low permeability due to molecular pore tension. Statement III is false because clay is porous.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":99,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\nI. Without the atmosphere, temperature would be well below freezing point everywhere on the Earth's surface.\\nII. Heat absorbed and trapped by the atmosphere maintains our planet's average temperature.\\nIII. Atmosphere's gases, like carbon dioxide, are particularly good at absorbing and trapping radiation.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["I and III only","I and II only","I, II and III","II and III only"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> The natural greenhouse effect driven by atmospheric gases (like $CO_2$ and $H_2O$ vapor) traps outgoing longwave terrestrial radiation, maintaining Earth's average surface temperature at around $+15^circ text{C}$ rather than $-18^circ\\text{C}$.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2025,"paper":"GS1","question":100,"q":"Consider the following statements about the Rashtriya Gokul Mission:\\nI. It is important for the upliftment of rural poor as majority of low producing indigenous animals are with small and marginal farmers and landless labourers.\\nII. It was initiated to promote indigenous cattle and buffalo rearing and conservation in a scientific and holistic manner.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both statements are correct:</b> The Rashtriya Gokul Mission was launched to conserve and develop indigenous bovine breeds in a focused, scientific manner, supporting the rural economy where the majority of indigenous livestock are held by small and marginal farmers.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"}]`);export{e as default};